# This repository runs with `core.autocrlf=true` and has no repository-root
# `.gitattributes`, so a fresh worktree otherwise receives CRLF and changes every
# byte this crate's gates read. Three sibling crates already carry this file for
# exactly that reason; `grapt-api` did not, and R1 HOLD-2 is what that cost.
#
# `tests/host_architecture.rs` reads the production modules with `include_str!`
# and scans the bytes ON DISK. On a CRLF checkout its test-module stripper found
# nothing to strip, so `admission.rs`'s `#[cfg(test)]` module — which legitimately
# calls `tokio::time::timeout` in a witness — was scanned as production source and
# the clock-placement gate fired on test code: 107 passed / 1 failed on a fresh
# default-config clone against 108 / 0 / 0 on an LF one, same commit, same
# toolchain, same machine.
#
# The stripper is independently newline-agnostic now, so this file is not the
# only thing standing between the gate and a false red. Both are here on purpose:
# the attribute pins what the gates read, and the stripper stops the gates
# depending on it. A checkout contract that is the sole defence is one that fails
# silently the first time someone clones with a different configuration.
#
# `tests/**` also covers the trybuild `.stderr` probes, which are byte-compared
# and would redden on a CRLF checkout with no content change.
src/** text eol=lf
tests/** text eol=lf
mutations/** text eol=lf

# The residuals are cited BY DIGEST in this lane's delivery receipt, so their
# bytes have to be the same for the reviewer as for the author. R1 measured
# exactly this drift before the rest of this file existed: a fresh default-config
# clone reported `R4HH_RESIDUALS.md` at 12,662 B against the admitted 12,448 B,
# the difference being precisely its line endings, and the reviewer had to
# reconfigure and re-check-out to reproduce the digest the receipt named. A cited
# hash that only holds on the machine that authored it is not evidence.
*.md text eol=lf
